---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="What ImageIO gives you free — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="One call to Apple's image library did far more for LightPhotos than I knew. When I moved to other platforms, I saw exactly how much."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-07-20">July 20, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>What ImageIO gives you free, and what it costs to leave</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          One call to Apple's ImageIO did far more for LightPhotos than I knew.
          When I moved to Linux, Windows and the browser, I saw exactly how
          much.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 5 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          On the Mac, one ImageIO call made RAW photos load fast. On Linux,
          Windows and the browser, I had to rebuild that work by hand. My
          version is slower, cannot read HEIC and needs a patched library. The
          hardest part was making photos look the same as on the Mac. A
          platform library does more than you think, and you only see it when
          you leave.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <figure class="lp-figure lp-try" id="imageio-demo">
        <div class="lp-try-controls" role="group" aria-label="Platform">
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-plat="mac" aria-pressed="true">macOS, ImageIO</button>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-plat="other" aria-pressed="false">Linux, Windows, browser</button>
        </div>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 286" role="img" aria-label="Interactive: seven jobs of reading a photo file, and who does each job on the selected platform.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">THE JOB</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="216" y="12" data-plat-label>ON THE MAC: ONE IMAGEIO CALL DOES IT</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="200" y="22" width="439" height="260" rx="8" data-one-call></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="49">Find the RAW preview</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="200" y="30" width="439" height="28" rx="5" data-row-box opacity="0"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="216" y="48" data-row="0">Built in, 11 ms</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="85">Shrink as it decodes</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="200" y="66" width="439" height="28" rx="5" data-row-box opacity="0"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="216" y="84" data-row="1">170 to 210 ms to screen size</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="121">Match Apple&#39;s look</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="200" y="102" width="439" height="28" rx="5" data-row-box opacity="0"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="216" y="120" data-row="2">Built in</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="157">Reduce noise</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="200" y="138" width="439" height="28" rx="5" data-row-box opacity="0"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="216" y="156" data-row="3">Automatic</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="193">Turn the photo upright</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="200" y="174" width="439" height="28" rx="5" data-row-box opacity="0"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="216" y="192" data-row="4">Built in</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="229">Read HEIC</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="200" y="210" width="439" height="28" rx="5" data-row-box opacity="0"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="216" y="228" data-row="5">Yes</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="265">Read Linear DNG</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="200" y="246" width="439" height="28" rx="5" data-row-box opacity="0"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="216" y="264" data-row="6">Cannot decode it</text>
        </svg>
        <p class="lp-try-readout" aria-live="polite">One call does six of the seven jobs. It cannot open a Linear DNG, and I cannot patch it.</p>
        <figcaption>
          Switch platforms to see what I had to build to replace one function
          argument.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        When I moved LightPhotos off the Mac, I expected the window, graphics
        and file dialogs to be hard. The hard part was reading an image
        file.
      </p>

      <h2>The one call</h2>
      <p>
        ImageIO is Apple's built-in image library. One of its functions means
        "give me this image, already shrunk to approximately this size". The
        size is part of the request.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a camera RAW file, that makes a very large difference. A RAW file
        holds the raw data from the sensor, and to turn that data into full
        colour takes a lot of work. But almost every RAW file also carries a
        small JPEG preview for the camera screen. If you ask ImageIO for a
        small version, it uses that preview and skips the sensor processing.
        On Sony ARW files, I measured 11 ms instead of 170 to 210.
      </p>

      <h2>No other platform has that function</h2>
      <p>
        I found no ready-made equivalent anywhere, so I built it. Most RAW files are TIFF files
        underneath. A TIFF file is a list of labelled entries (tags), and the
        preview sits at a known spot in that list. If that fails, rawler, the
        open source RAW library I use, demosaics the sensor data.
      </p>

      <h2>Two steps in the wrong order</h2>
      <p>
        That 680 ms started as 1.80 seconds. My code did noise reduction on
        the full-size sensor image, and only shrank it afterwards.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure lp-try" id="order-demo">
        <div class="lp-try-controls" role="group" aria-label="Step order">
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-order="before" aria-pressed="true">Denoise, then shrink</button>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-order="after" aria-pressed="false">Shrink, then denoise</button>
        </div>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 110" role="img" aria-label="Interactive: the order of the denoise and shrink steps, and the time and peak memory for each order.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">A 2560 PX PREVIEW FROM A 24 MP SONY ARW</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="26" width="142" height="32" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="71" y="46" text-anchor="middle">Decode sensor</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M146 36 L146 48 L156 42 Z M306 36 L306 48 L316 42 Z"></path>
          <g data-step="denoise" transform="translate(160 0)">
            <rect class="lp-fig-box lp-fig-bad-stroke" x="0" y="26" width="142" height="32" rx="6"></rect>
            <text class="lp-fig-s" x="71" y="46" text-anchor="middle">Denoise at 24 MP</text>
          </g>
          <g data-step="shrink" transform="translate(320 0)">
            <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="26" width="142" height="32" rx="6"></rect>
            <text class="lp-fig-s" x="71" y="46" text-anchor="middle">Shrink to 2560</text>
          </g>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="0" y="74" width="462" height="10" rx="5" data-time-bar></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="104" data-time>1.80 s &middot; peak 1.39 GB</text>
        </svg>
        <p class="lp-try-readout" aria-live="polite">Noise reduction runs on all 24 MP. 1.80 s per photo.</p>
        <figcaption>
          A two-line swap. At full size, the output is identical in every byte.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Swapping the two steps saved 1.1 seconds per photo. Check the order of
        your processing steps.
      </p>

      <h2>The other costs</h2>
      <p>
        File formats were the second cost. ImageIO reads HEIC, the iPhone
        format. Nobody has written a HEIC reader in pure Rust, the language
        LightPhotos uses. A C library would add build tools on every platform,
        and I want those builds simple. So HEIC only works on the Mac, and you
        can see that as a user.
      </p>
      <p>
        The third cost was a library that did not run in a browser. The RAW
        library reads the system clock to log load times. Under WebAssembly,
        no clock exists behind that call, so it crashes. My build now
        downloads the library and applies a four-line patch. Cargo, the Rust
        build tool, cannot apply a patch for only the browser build, so every
        build uses the patched copy.
      </p>
      <p>
        A platform library has a price: speed, file formats, other systems,
        and bugs you cannot fix. I guessed three of those four wrong. Only the
        replacement, timed against the original, told me the real price.
      </p>

      <h2>Making the other platforms look like the Mac</h2>
      <p>
        The photo also had to look the same as on the Mac. That was the
        hardest part of the move. Bigger photo projects have camera profiles,
        each tuned for one camera model. I only have the basic colour data
        that rawler keeps for each camera. My first version looked flat and
        approximately 1.45 times darker than the Mac.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Use what the camera recorded.</strong> I apply the saved
          white balance, then convert to screen colours with the numbers
          rawler has for that camera.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Handle highlights the way Apple does.</strong> I pull back
          colours that are too bright for the screen gently instead of cutting
          them off. The very brightest tones go to pure white, as in ImageIO.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Trim the edges.</strong> I did not trim all of the sensor
          border pixels, and those dark edges made the browser version visibly
          darker.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Turn the photo the right way up.</strong> The RAW library
          reported every photo as upright, so I read the rotation myself.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reduce noise.</strong> ImageIO does this automatically. I
          add a fixed amount.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Brightness was the biggest piece. Tuning by eye got close but never
        looked right. So I wrote a small tool that opens
        the same RAW file with ImageIO and with my code, and compares how
        bright the results are. Across 148 Sony files, it aligned my output
        with Apple's at 17 points from black to white. I kept the middle value
        at each point and fitted a curve. On the ten files I checked, my
        version had been 1.34 to 1.51 times darker. With the curve, it is
        within 2% either way.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is still not a perfect match. I only fitted the curve on Sony
        files, because I shoot with Sony, so other cameras may not match as
        closely. The curve fixes brightness and contrast, not colour.
        Fujifilm cameras with X-Trans sensors also get a softer result than on
        the Mac.
      </p>

      <h2>Apple's decoder is not always right</h2>
      <p>
        My plan for an accuracy check was to open a test file both ways and
        treat Apple's result as correct. Apple's decoder could not open the
        file. It cannot read Linear DNG files at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I made an artificial DNG file that holds a known mathematical
        gradient, and I calculated every pixel by hand. The Rust decoder
        matched exactly, with zero mismatches across 147,456 samples. If two
        decoders agree, that only tells me that they agree. When one matches
        numbers I worked out myself, I know that it is right.
      </p>

      <h2>I would use it again</h2>
      <p>
        I still use ImageIO on the Mac. It gives decades of format support, a
        fast shortcut I would not have thought to build, and correct results
        on reverse-engineered camera formats. I rebuilt a small piece of it,
        and now I respect it much more. Thank you, Apple.
      </p>
      <p>
        The price is that ImageIO only works on Apple's platforms, and you
        cannot fix its bugs. I cannot patch the Linear DNG gap. The only
        workaround is to ship a second decoder.
      </p>

      <nav class="lp-article-nav" aria-label="More posts">
        <a class="lp-article-nav-prev" href="/blogs/how-a-photo-gets-on-screen.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Back</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">How a photo gets on screen</span>
        </a>
        <a class="lp-article-nav-next" href="/blogs/where-your-data-lives.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Next</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">Your data lives where you are allowed to write</span>
        </a>
      </nav>
    </article>
  </main>
  <script>
    // Each pair is [macOS with ImageIO, Linux/Windows/browser], from the post.
    const JOBS = [
      [["Built in, 11 ms"], ["I read the TIFF and EXIF tags by hand"]],
      [["170 to 210 ms to screen size"], ["680 ms, after a fix (it was 1.80 s)"]],
      [["Built in"], ["A brightness curve fitted on 148 Sony files"]],
      [["Automatic"], ["A fixed amount"]],
      [["Built in"], ["I read the rotation from the metadata"]],
      [["Yes"], ["No pure-Rust decoder exists", "bad"]],
      [["Cannot decode it", "bad"], ["Byte-exact against a hand-worked answer"]],
    ];

    const pick = (fig, attr, onPick) => {
      const btns = fig.querySelectorAll(`[${attr}]`);
      btns.forEach((btn) =>
        btn.addEventListener("click", () => {
          btns.forEach((b) => b.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(b === btn)));
          onPick(btn.getAttribute(attr));
        })
      );
    };

    const platFig = document.getElementById("imageio-demo");
    if (platFig) {
      const readout = platFig.querySelector(".lp-try-readout");
      pick(platFig, "data-plat", (plat) => {
        const mac = plat === "mac";
        platFig.querySelector("[data-one-call]").setAttribute("opacity", mac ? "1" : "0");
        platFig.querySelectorAll("[data-row-box]").forEach((r) => r.setAttribute("opacity", mac ? "0" : "1"));
        platFig.querySelector("[data-plat-label]").textContent = mac
          ? "ON THE MAC: ONE IMAGEIO CALL DOES IT"
          : "ELSEWHERE: CODE I WROTE, JOB BY JOB";
        JOBS.forEach((job, i) => {
          const [text, tone] = job[mac ? 0 : 1];
          const el = platFig.querySelector(`[data-row="${i}"]`);
          el.textContent = text;
          el.setAttribute("class", tone === "bad" ? "lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" : "lp-fig-s");
        });
        readout.textContent = mac
          ? "One call does six of the seven jobs. It cannot open a Linear DNG, and I cannot patch it."
          : "I rebuilt six of the seven jobs by hand. HEIC still works only on the Mac.";
      });
    }

    const orderFig = document.getElementById("order-demo");
    if (orderFig) {
      const readout = orderFig.querySelector(".lp-try-readout");
      pick(orderFig, "data-order", (order) => {
        const late = order === "before";
        const denoise = orderFig.querySelector('[data-step="denoise"]');
        denoise.setAttribute("transform", `translate(${late ? 160 : 320} 0)`);
        orderFig.querySelector('[data-step="shrink"]').setAttribute("transform", `translate(${late ? 320 : 160} 0)`);
        denoise.querySelector("rect").setAttribute("class", late ? "lp-fig-box lp-fig-bad-stroke" : "lp-fig-box-accent");
        denoise.querySelector("text").textContent = late ? "Denoise at 24 MP" : "Denoise at 2560";
        const bar = orderFig.querySelector("[data-time-bar]");
        bar.setAttribute("width", late ? "462" : "175");
        bar.setAttribute("class", late ? "lp-fig-bar-dim" : "lp-fig-bar");
        orderFig.querySelector("[data-time]").textContent = late
          ? "1.80 s · peak 1.39 GB"
          : "0.68 s · peak 1.21 GB";
        readout.textContent = late
          ? "Noise reduction runs on all 24 MP. 1.80 s per photo."
          : "Noise reduction runs on the 2560 px image. 0.68 s per photo, 1.1 s saved.";
      });
    }
  </script>
</Base>
